# /etc/wifi.conf -- the WiFi network to join (WPA2-PSK).
#
# No network is configured in this image. The simple way to add one is the
# shell command
#
#     wifi connect <ssid> <passphrase>
#
# which writes /etc/wifi.conf (readable by its owner only), waits for the DHCP
# lease and prints the address. The network is joined again after every reboot
# until you run `wifi disconnect`, which removes the file. `wifi status` shows
# the wanted network, the radio state and the address; `wifi scan` lists the
# access points in range.
#
# To configure it by hand instead, copy this file to /etc/wifi.conf, fill in
# the two lines below and remove their leading "#". The WiFi interface (wl)
# re-reads the file every few seconds, so a change takes effect without a
# reboot. One key=value per line; '#' starts a comment; spaces around the value
# are ignored. The passphrase is 8-63 characters and may contain '=' and
# spaces (psh passes quotes through literally, so a passphrase with a space
# has to be written here rather than given to `wifi connect`). Not supported
# yet: an ssid containing a space, and a 64-digit hex key.
#
#     ssid=MyNetwork
#     psk=my secret passphrase
#
# Ethernet stays the default route while it has an address; WiFi takes the
# default route when Ethernet has none (e.g. no cable).
